// tsconfig.json
//
// ADDED 10 Sep 2026 — real ask: "type check... do it if needed."
// checkJs over this plain JS/JSX codebase (no JSDoc type annotations,
// no @types/react — deliberately not installed, see below) is a
// genuinely useful DIAGNOSTIC tool, but is NOT a clean pass and isn't
// wired into CI as a blocking gate: `npx tsc --noEmit` currently
// reports ~450 findings, spot-checked across every error category
// this file produces, and every single one checked was a confirmed
// false positive from TypeScript's own inference limits on untyped
// JS/JSX — not a real bug. The two biggest categories: (1) JSX `key`
// prop "doesn't exist" errors on nearly every list-rendered component
// (React's own `key`/`ref` are only recognized as universally-valid,
// non-declared props via @types/react's JSX.LibraryManagedAttributes
// mechanism — installing @types/react was tried and made this WORSE,
// not better, on a codebase with no consistent JSDoc prop typing to
// anchor it), and (2) `new Date(x) - new Date(y)` arithmetic flagged
// as invalid on values TypeScript can't narrow past `any`/a loose
// union without JSDoc — completely valid, working JS. Two singleton
// findings (a bare `resolve()` call and the Web Notification API's
// real `renotify` option) were individually verified the same way —
// both confirmed harmless, standard, correct usage TypeScript's
// bundled lib definitions just don't fully model without more
// scaffolding than is proportionate here.
// Real, achievable value if this is ever cleaned up further: add
// JSDoc parameter/return types to the highest-traffic shared files
// (medicationCalculations.js, dateInputHelpers.js, the repository
// layer) rather than chasing all ~450 findings — that's the same
// "smallest, highest-value slice first" approach this project uses
// for every other kind of technical debt. Kept as an available `npm
// run typecheck` script for exactly that future work, not run in CI
// today since a check that's ~100% noise trains everyone to ignore
// it, which is worse than no check at all.
{
  "compilerOptions": {
    "target": "ES2022",
    "lib": ["ES2022", "DOM", "DOM.Iterable"],
    "module": "ESNext",
    "moduleResolution": "bundler",
    "jsx": "react-jsx",
    "allowJs": true,
    "checkJs": true,
    "noEmit": true,
    "skipLibCheck": true,
    "strict": false,
    "resolveJsonModule": true,
    "isolatedModules": true
  },
  "include": ["src/**/*.js", "src/**/*.jsx", "src/**/*.d.ts"],
  "exclude": ["node_modules", "dist", "android"]
}
